spec: specs/intent-spans.md — typed spans from token vectors, and the encoder that keeps them

why: okay-chat measured (its specs/meaning.md, 317 live turns) that the
  production encoder's TOKEN vectors — which mean pooling throws away —
  find the place and the time of a message at 93% / 86% agreement with
  hand-written extractors and find what those cannot name, with no fitting.
  Three things it needs from the platform: a Token (a vector with a
  location) in okay-rag; a Spans tier in okay-intent, pure, fitted from
  prototypes embedded IN CONTEXT, shipped as data through Fitted; and an
  okay-onnx module (operator's name) — the direct ONNX session that gives
  the pooled vector and the token vectors from ONE forward pass.
